…the root of all transforming influence is purified, and government capable of winning over those nearby is established. With all worthy and talented men lending their assistance, every undertaking can flourish, and the benefits of caring generously for those nearby will follow of themselves. In deliberations at court, there will be no departure from the Way. Ministers will encounter no obstruction in supporting and guiding their ruler, and all who rest secure in their positions and conscientiously discharge their duties will enjoy harmony and contentment at an enlightened court. When these policies are extended throughout the realm, there will be no disruption of affairs. The people will not be distressed by levies and exactions, and all who pursue their occupations securely and support themselves through their own efforts will live contentedly in a tranquil age. Could any of those nearby remain unsettled? Ah! By devoting oneself wholeheartedly to treasuring the worthy, one achieves the result of bringing peace to the people. Truly, no treasure under Heaven surpasses this.

Yet consider this: the Book of Changes says, “The sage’s great treasure is his position,” whereas the Duke of Shao says, “What he treasures is only worthy men.” Treasuring worthy men is precisely the means of treasuring one’s position. In King Wu’s age, there were ten ministers who brought order: when did he ever fail to treasure them? All under Heaven rejoiced greatly; they enjoyed far more than mere peace. Even accepting a mastiff as tribute might seem harmless. Why, then, did the Duke of Shao so earnestly warn him against it? He feared that an incipient tendency might grow, and guarded against it beforehand. Because King Wu was able to act on this counsel, worthy and talented men attached themselves to him ever more closely, and the people became ever more secure. Ah! This is a mirror for rulers and ministers for all ages.

Book of Odes

In the silkworm month, we prune the mulberries;
We take those axes and hatchets
To cut the branches reaching high and far,
And bend down the young mulberry shoots.
In the seventh month, the shrike calls;
In the eighth month, we begin spinning.
We dye the cloth dark and yellow;
Our vermilion is brilliantly bright,
For the lord’s sons’ lower garments.
In the fourth month, the yao plant comes into ear;
In the fifth month, the cicadas call;
In the eighth month …
